            "kind": "NO_CODE_CHANGE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "These would be generated from pid control? Does pid-control have a concept of a failed fan?  i thought people just set thresholds to determine when a fan was misbehaving.  or does it watch thresholds"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Good question.  Maybe I don't need this.\n\nWe seem to have a few cases:\n- The fan is spinning below thresholds.  This should be reported by dbus-sensors as a fan threshold issue.\n- The fan isn't spinning at the speed requested (or is 0).  Maybe this could be handled by thresholds also or maybe pid-control should explicitly monitor for this.\n- There are secondary hardware necessary to get the fan to work, which is raising a failure signal (usually a GPIO).  The two I see right now in our design are:\n    1. The io-expander to the fan controller isn't working and/or raised an interrupt. (I need to look at this closer because I don't think this is actually a fan failure)\n    2. There is an \"efuse\" to prevent over-current and it has blown and raised an interrupt.\n\nThe \"efuse\" one I could probably map to the power-rail failures I put in a different diff."
                }
            ],
